{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.0e893b889a","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"pair-ic4-155","passage":"<p>The water heater ______ family took cold showers for a week.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["broke therefore, the","broke, therefore the","broke, therefore, the","broke; therefore, the"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Both “The water heater broke” and “The family took cold showers for a week” are independent clauses (each could stand alone as a sentence). “Therefore” is a transition word, not a conjunction, so it can't join two independent clauses with only commas. Use a <b>semicolon before “therefore” and a comma after it</b>."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.35df6ea467","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"pair-ic4-160","passage":"<p>The dancer was ______ performed with grace.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["injured; however, she","injured, however she","injured, however, she","injured however, she"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Both “The dancer was injured” and “She performed with grace” are independent clauses (each could stand alone as a sentence). “However” is a transition word, not a conjunction, so it can't join two independent clauses with only commas. Use a <b>semicolon before “however” and a comma after it</b>."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.ec3e204894","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-list-4","passage":"<p>The recipe contest finalists came from Dayton, Ohio; Mobile, ______ Boise, Idaho.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Alabama: and","Alabama and","Alabama, and","Alabama; and"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Each item in this list already contains a comma, so <b>semicolons</b> must separate the items to keep them distinct. The pattern set by the first semicolon must continue before “and.”"},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.1e2f6640e7","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-list-5","passage":"<p>The team's top scorers were Jordan, a forward; Kim, a ______ Sam, a defender.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["midfielder, and","midfielder: and","midfielder; and","midfielder and"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Each item in this list already contains a comma, so <b>semicolons</b> must separate the items to keep them distinct. The pattern set by the first semicolon must continue before “and.”"},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.e9d812c483","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-list-6","passage":"<p>The garden is divided into three sections: herbs, such as basil and mint; vegetables, such as beans and ______ flowers, such as zinnias and marigolds.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["squash; and","squash, and","squash and","squash: and"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Each item in this list already contains a comma, so <b>semicolons</b> must separate the items to keep them distinct. The pattern set by the first semicolon must continue before “and.”"},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.7b9f6170e4","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-list-8","passage":"<p>The award winners included a poet from Lagos, Nigeria; a painter from Hanoi, ______ a composer from Oslo, Norway.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Vietnam, and","Vietnam and","Vietnam; and","Vietnam: and"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Each item in this list already contains a comma, so <b>semicolons</b> must separate the items to keep them distinct. The pattern set by the first semicolon must continue before “and.”"},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.ed0632f016","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-list-13","passage":"<p>The new menu items are soup, served with fresh bread; salad, topped with roasted ______ pasta, made with local tomatoes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["nuts; and","nuts, and","nuts: and","nuts and"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Each item in this list already contains a comma, so <b>semicolons</b> must separate the items to keep them distinct. The pattern set by the first semicolon must continue before “and.”"},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.01c6e160bb","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-iq-1","passage":"<p>The students asked whether the field trip would be canceled because of the ______ teacher promised to decide by morning.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["rain? their","rain, their","rain? Their","rain. Their"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The first sentence reports a question indirectly (for example, “wondered why” or “asked whether”) instead of asking it directly, so it ends with a <b>period</b>, not a question mark. A comma would create a comma splice."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.0ebe17a4bc","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-iq-7","passage":"<p>Astronomers are trying to determine whether the planet has an ______ from the new telescope should help.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["atmosphere, data","atmosphere. Data","atmosphere? data","atmosphere? Data"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The first sentence reports a question indirectly (for example, “wondered why” or “asked whether”) instead of asking it directly, so it ends with a <b>period</b>, not a question mark. A comma would create a comma splice."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.60799af4e1","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-iq-11","passage":"<p>Scientists are studying how octopuses solve complex ______ findings could reveal new insights about animal intelligence.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["puzzles? Their","puzzles. Their","puzzles, their","puzzles? their"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The first sentence reports a question indirectly (for example, “wondered why” or “asked whether”) instead of asking it directly, so it ends with a <b>period</b>, not a question mark. A comma would create a comma splice."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.1a0ff1604f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-iq-12","passage":"<p>The coach wondered whether the team was ready for the ______ players answered with a decisive win.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["championship, the","championship. The","championship? The","championship? the"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The first sentence reports a question indirectly (for example, “wondered why” or “asked whether”) instead of asking it directly, so it ends with a <b>period</b>, not a question mark. A comma would create a comma splice."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.3.4abb14d431","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":3,"group":"bnd-iq-13","passage":"<p>Readers often ask why the novel ends so ______ author has never explained her choice.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["abruptly? The","abruptly. The","abruptly, the","abruptly? the"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The first sentence reports a question indirectly (for example, “wondered why” or “asked whether”) instead of asking it directly, so it ends with a <b>period</b>, not a question mark. A comma would create a comma splice."}
